TASK 3 – FROM SKETCH TO LAYOUT 
Task 3.1 – Choose the Layout Managers 
The Campus Library Search Screen requires a layout that maintains the size of the search row and navigation buttons while allowing the results area to expand when the window is resized.

The most suitable design is a combination of BorderLayout and FlowLayout.
1. Layout Manager Selection
S.No.	Container / Region	Layout Manager	Justification
1	Main JFrame	BorderLayout	Divides the window into North, Center and South regions.
2	Title Panel	FlowLayout	Places the title at the top without unnecessary stretching.
3	Search Panel	FlowLayout	Arranges the text field, category Choice and Search button horizontally.
4	Results Panel	BorderLayout	Allows the results area to occupy all available centre space and expand when resized.
5	Navigation Panel	FlowLayout	Keeps Prev, Next and Reserve buttons together at the bottom.
6	Status Panel	FlowLayout	Displays the status message at the bottom.

2. Explanation of Layout Choices
BorderLayout:
- Divides the main window into five regions: North, South, East, West and Center.
- The Center region receives the extra available space when the window is resized.
- Therefore, the results area grows while the other regions retain their preferred sizes.
FlowLayout:
- Arranges components from left to right.
- Maintains the preferred sizes of buttons and other components.
- Suitable for the search row and navigation buttons.
GridLayout:
- Arranges components in equal-sized rows and columns.
- Not selected because it would unnecessarily stretch components and make the search controls larger when the window is resized.
CardLayout:
- Displays one component at a time.
- Useful for switching between different screens, but unnecessary for this library search screen.
Selected design
 Nested BorderLayout and FlowLayout panels provide the required resizing behaviour with a simple component structure.
