Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
1. Alternative Approach: GridBagLayout
Instead of using nested BorderLayout and FlowLayout, the entire screen can be designed using GridBagLayout.

GridBagLayout allows components to be arranged in rows and columns with different sizes and resizing weights.

How the design changes:
- The JFrame uses GridBagLayout.
- The title occupies the first row.
- The search controls occupy the second row.
- The results area occupies the centre and receives a higher weighty value.
- Navigation buttons and status message occupy the bottom rows.
- fill, weightx and weighty control component resizing.
Example:

JFrame frame = new JFrame("Library Search");

frame.setLayout(new GridBagLayout());

GridBagConstraints gbc = new GridBagConstraints();

gbc.fill = GridBagConstraints.HORIZONTAL;
gbc.weightx = 1.0;

JLabel title = new JLabel("CAMPUS LIBRARY SEARCH");
gbc.gridx = 0;
gbc.gridy = 0;
gbc.gridwidth = 3;
gbc.weighty = 0;
frame.add(title, gbc);

JPanel searchPanel = new JPanel(new FlowLayout());
searchPanel.add(new JTextField(20));
searchPanel.add(new JButton("Search"));

gbc.gridy = 1;
gbc.weighty = 0;
frame.add(searchPanel, gbc);

JTextArea results = new JTextArea();
JScrollPane scroll = new JScrollPane(results);

gbc.gridy = 2;
gbc.weighty = 1.0;
gbc.fill = GridBagConstraints.BOTH;
frame.add(scroll, gbc);

JPanel bottomPanel = new JPanel();
bottomPanel.add(new JButton("Prev"));
bottomPanel.add(new JButton("Next"));
bottomPanel.add(new JButton("Reserve"));

gbc.gridy = 3;
gbc.weighty = 0;
gbc.fill = GridBagConstraints.HORIZONTAL;
frame.add(bottomPanel, gbc);



The results area expands because its weighty is set to 1.0, while the other rows have a weight of zero.
2. Alternative Approach: Absolute Positioning
Another approach is absolute positioning using:
setLayout(null);


Each component is positioned manually using setBounds(x, y, width, height).

Example:

JFrame frame = new JFrame("Library Search");

frame.setLayout(null);
frame.setSize(800, 500);

JLabel title = new JLabel("CAMPUS LIBRARY SEARCH");
title.setBounds(250, 20, 300, 30);

JTextField search = new JTextField();
search.setBounds(50, 70, 250, 30);

JButton searchButton = new JButton("Search");
searchButton.setBounds(550, 70, 100, 30);

JTextArea results = new JTextArea();
results.setBounds(50, 120, 700, 250);

JButton prev = new JButton("Prev");
prev.setBounds(250, 390, 80, 30);

JButton next = new JButton("Next");
next.setBounds(350, 390, 80, 30);

JButton reserve = new JButton("Reserve");
reserve.setBounds(450, 390, 100, 30);

frame.add(title);
frame.add(search);
frame.add(searchButton);
frame.add(results);
frame.add(prev);
frame.add(next);
frame.add(reserve);

frame.setVisible(true);



In this approach, every component receives manually specified coordinates and dimensions. When the window is resized, the components do not automatically adjust their positions or sizes.
3. Time Complexity Analysis
Let n represent the total number of GUI components.
Layout approach	Layout computation time	Explanation
Nested BorderLayout and FlowLayout	O(n)	Each container arranges its child components.
GridBagLayout	O(n) for a fixed grid configuration	Calculates component positions, constraints and sizes.
Absolute positioning	O(1) initial placement per component, O(n) overall	Each component is assigned its bounds individually.

For nested layouts:
\[
T(n)=O(n)
\]

For GridBagLayout:
\[
T(n)=O(n)
\]

For absolute positioning:
\[
T(n)=O(n)
\]

The overall initial placement of n components requires O(n) work. Absolute positioning avoids much of the layout-manager calculation, but resizing requires additional manual calculations if components must adjust.

These are simplified asymptotic estimates; actual Swing layout costs depend on container nesting, constraints and component measurements.
4. Space Complexity Analysis
Space complexity depends on the number of components, containers and layout constraints.
Approach	Container objects	Space complexity
Nested BorderLayout and FlowLayout	Multiple JPanel containers	O(n) including components
GridBagLayout	Can use fewer JPanel containers	O(n) including components and constraints
Absolute positioning	Can use a single JFrame	O(n) including components and bounds

For the proposed nested layout, the number of panels is fixed, so the additional container overhead is O(1). However, the complete GUI requires O(n) space for its components.

GridBagLayout may reduce the number of nested panels but requires additional constraint objects.

Absolute positioning can use fewer containers, but still requires O(n) space to store the component references and bounds.
5. Final Comparison
Parameter	Nested layouts	GridBagLayout	Absolute positioning
Implementation	Simple	More complex	Simple initially
Resizing	Automatic	Automatic with constraints	Not automatic
Component positioning	Managed automatically	Constraint-based	Manual
Layout computation	O(n)	O(n) simplified	O(n) overall
Space complexity	O(n)	O(n)	O(n)
Maintainability	High	High with careful constraints	Low
Responsiveness	Good	Very good	Poor without manual resizing

Final Conclusion
Nested BorderLayout and FlowLayout are the most suitable choices for the Campus Library Search Screen because they provide automatic resizing with a simple and maintainable structure. GridBagLayout offers greater control but requires more configuration. Absolute positioning is not recommended because components do not adjust automatically when the window is resized.

Therefore, the nested layout approach is preferred for this assignment.
